<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  An App Group, for a real reason and a useful side effect.

	  The real reason: the exporter is two processes (a root LaunchDaemon and an
	  unprivileged LaunchAgent — see P1_CAPTURE_VERIFICATION.md) and they have to
	  share the pin store and the identity seed. A group container is the macOS
	  way to do that without inventing a path convention.

	  The side effect that matters right now: App Groups is a capability that
	  REQUIRES a provisioning profile. macOS development signing otherwise needs
	  no profile at all, so Xcode never contacts the Developer portal and never
	  registers an App ID — which is why the first build here signed cleanly and
	  created nothing. With this present, automatic signing has to go and ask,
	  and the App ID appears.

	  That App ID is the prerequisite for the Capability Requests tab, which does
	  not exist until an App ID does.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.otti83.airusbhub</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
